examples.question_text:
Two fair four-sided dice are rolled independently. Each die is numbered $1,2,3,4$. Use an array to find the probability that:
(a) their sum is at least $6$;
(b) their scores differ by $2$;
(c) the first score is greater than the second.

A student has probability $0.6$ of passing a maths test and $0.75$ of passing a science test. The results are independent. Draw a probability tree and find the probability that the student:
(a) passes both tests;
(b) passes science but fails maths;
(c) passes exactly one test.

examples.solution_text:
Let $P$ mean pass and $F$ mean fail. Independence keeps the science probabilities unchanged after either maths result.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},x=1cm,y=1cm]
\node at (1.4,2.8) {Maths};
\node at (4.4,2.8) {Science};
\draw (0,0)--(2,1.4) node[midway,above left] {$0.6$};
\draw (0,0)--(2,-1.4) node[midway,below left] {$0.4$};
\node[fill=white,inner sep=2pt] at (2,1.4) {$P$};
\node[fill=white,inner sep=2pt] at (2,-1.4) {$F$};
\draw (2,1.4)--(5,2.2) node[midway,above] {$0.75$};
\draw (2,1.4)--(5,0.6) node[midway,below] {$0.25$};
\draw (2,-1.4)--(5,-0.6) node[midway,above] {$0.75$};
\draw (2,-1.4)--(5,-2.2) node[midway,below] {$0.25$};
\node[right] at (5,2.2) {$P\quad PP$};
\node[right] at (5,0.6) {$F\quad PF$};
\node[right] at (5,-0.6) {$P\quad FP$};
\node[right] at (5,-2.2) {$F\quad FF$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(a) Multiply along the $PP$ path.
$\begin{aligned}P(PP)&=0.6\times0.75\\&=0.45\end{aligned}$

(b) Follow the $FP$ path.
$\begin{aligned}P(FP)&=0.4\times0.75\\&=0.30\end{aligned}$

(c) Add the two paths with exactly one pass.
$\begin{aligned}P(\text{exactly one pass})&=P(PF)+P(FP)\\&=0.6\times0.25+0.4\times0.75\\&=0.45\end{aligned}$

examples.question_text:
A spinner has five equal sections: three red and two blue. It is spun three times independently. Find the probability of:
(a) three reds;
(b) exactly two reds.

A bag contains $4$ red and $5$ blue counters. Two counters are drawn at random without replacement. Draw a probability tree and find the probability of:
(a) two red counters;
(b) one counter of each colour;
(c) two counters of the same colour;
(d) at least one red counter.

examples.solution_text:
After the first draw, eight counters remain. The second-stage probabilities depend on which colour was removed.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},x=1cm,y=1cm]
\node at (1.3,3) {Draw 1};
\node at (4.3,3) {Draw 2};
\draw (0,0)--(2,1.5) node[midway,above left] {$\frac49$};
\draw (0,0)--(2,-1.5) node[midway,below left] {$\frac59$};
\node[fill=white,inner sep=2pt] at (2,1.5) {$R$};
\node[fill=white,inner sep=2pt] at (2,-1.5) {$B$};
\draw (2,1.5)--(5,2.4) node[midway,above] {$\frac38$};
\draw (2,1.5)--(5,0.6) node[midway,below] {$\frac58$};
\draw (2,-1.5)--(5,-0.6) node[midway,above] {$\frac48$};
\draw (2,-1.5)--(5,-2.4) node[midway,below] {$\frac48$};
\node[right] at (5,2.4) {$R\quad RR$};
\node[right] at (5,0.6) {$B\quad RB$};
\node[right] at (5,-0.6) {$R\quad BR$};
\node[right] at (5,-2.4) {$B\quad BB$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Multiply along each path.
$\begin{aligned}P(RR)&=\frac49\times\frac38=\frac{12}{72}\\P(RB)&=\frac49\times\frac58=\frac{20}{72}\\P(BR)&=\frac59\times\frac48=\frac{20}{72}\\P(BB)&=\frac59\times\frac48=\frac{20}{72}\end{aligned}$
These four path probabilities sum to $1$.

(a) $P(RR)=\frac{12}{72}=\frac16$.

(b)
$\begin{aligned}P(\text{one of each})&=P(RB)+P(BR)\\&=\frac{20}{72}+\frac{20}{72}\\&=\frac59\end{aligned}$

(c)
$\begin{aligned}P(\text{same colour})&=P(RR)+P(BB)\\&=\frac{12}{72}+\frac{20}{72}\\&=\frac49\end{aligned}$

(d) No red counters means the $BB$ path.
$\begin{aligned}P(\text{at least one red})&=1-P(BB)\\&=1-\frac{20}{72}\\&=\frac{13}{18}\end{aligned}$
